      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, so I went down a massive rabbit hole here. I\u0027ll give you the TLDR:\n* My first solution from a few weeks ago tried to use `autocxx` to just generate rust code for all C++ headers. It was a massive nightmare, and really not worth the hassle.\n* The current solution involves rust and C++ not knowing about each other, but they just happen to agree on certain FFI-compatible things.\n\nI found a happy middle ground which I think is the right way to go. The rust code generates a C++ header and source file that provide a shim to rust. This ensures that rust is compatible with the shim. If the shim is not compatible with the actual code (eg. the real function declares a Label* but we declared Label\u0026 in rust), then when you compile the shim itself, it will fail.\n\nSo don\u0027t review bridge.cc/bridge.h, they\u0027re generated files. Start by reading bridge.rs.",
